wmcliphist: only the last copy is given back, never a favorite

When the application that owned the CLIPBOARD went away, auto_take_up
published the first item of the history list. That list is reordered
by the saved history (big-wm-clipboard --dump put favorites first, so
after each reload a favorite was on top), so Ctrl+V pasted a favorite
or an older item instead of the last copy. On XWayland the owner is the
compositor's bridge, which drops the selection at every copy and every
close of a Wayland client: the favorite also went to the Wayland
clipboard (big-wm-clipboard --copy). Measured: 32 of 34 checks pasted
the favorite in a nested wlmaker, 11 of 203 on X11.

- The last copy is kept apart from the history (last_copy): what was
  copied last, or the item chosen from the menu. Only it is published
  again when the owner goes away.
- A copy that is not text (an image, files only) or that is a secret
  leaves no last copy: no older text comes back in its place.
- Nothing is published when wmcliphist starts: the saved history is in
  the menu and in Super+V, and is only pasted when chosen.
- The last copy follows what really happened: an application copying
  the text wmcliphist had published makes it the last copy again; text
  rejected by an "ignore" action stays out of it when copied twice; and
  an answer that arrives after a newer copy (owner generation) never
  becomes the last copy (an image followed by a slow text answer used to
  bring the text back).
- On XWayland nothing is published when the owner goes away (the owner
  is the bridge, not an application); big-wm-clipboard keeps the
  Wayland clipboard.

